SUMMARY:Essays on the Energy Transition in Electricity Markets
DTSTAMP:20260730T054413Z
DTSTART:20260812T210000Z
DESCRIPTION:Renewable energy is restructuring the generation mix in electri
	city markets\, simultaneously driving thermal retirement\, substantially s
	uppressing wholesale energy prices when and where they are generating\, an
	d redirecting investment toward new technologies such as battery storage t
	o compensate for their effects. This dissertation studies all three channe
	ls in the Electric Reliability Council of Texas (ERCOT) market. Each chapt
	er takes a different angle and methodology: structural estimation of retir
	ement decisions\, analytical equilibrium theory for renewable dynamics\, a
	nd reduced-form spatial analysis of investment composition. The first chap
	ter estimates a dynamic discrete choice model of retirement for natural ga
	s and coal-fired generators in ERCOT\, treating exit as an irreversible op
	timal stopping problem under forward-looking expectations. All 45 observed
	 exits in the estimation panel occur at strictly positive operating profit
	\, consistent with retirement as a forward-looking continuation-value deci
	sion rather than a current-period margin trigger. The retirement decision 
	in the model has two channels: a current-period flow payoff in which opera
	ting profit and age enter directly\, and a forward-looking continuation va
	lue that summarizes the expected path of future operating profit. Operatin
	g profit is constructed at unit resolution from a 15-minute spark-spread p
	ayoff that combines real-time nodal locational marginal prices (LMPs) and 
	fuel-cost indices (Henry Hub for gas units\, delivered coal costs for coal
	 units). Ancillary service revenue recovered from the ERCOT 60-Day Securit
	y-Constrained Economic Dispatch (SCED) record is added\, and pro-rated fix
	ed operating and maintenance costs are subtracted. Operating profit enters
	 the flow payoff positively and significantly (coefficient +0.653\, t-stat
	istic 3.15 under a random-effects specification). Generator age enters neg
	atively (-0.510)\, marginal under random effects and strongly significant 
	without them. Wind and solar shape retirement decisions through the operat
	ing-profit transition kernel rather than the flow payoff. Their persistenc
	e in the kernel transmits into continuation values\, which is how the mode
	l captures the effect of renewable trajectories on exit. Both kernel coeff
	icients are negative (wind -0.157\, solar -0.030)\, reflecting the merit-o
	rder erosion of incumbent operating profit\, with wind the stronger channe
	l and solar the weaker. Two counterfactual exercises explore the implicati
	ons of the estimated structural model. A wind-versus-solar dose-response v
	aries zonal renewable shares and shows how marginal renewable expansion re
	shapes the composition of the surviving fleet. A forward projection built 
	from the April 2026 ERCOT Generator Interconnection Status report calibrat
	es expected renewable buildout. Through 2035\, the fleet's existing age di
	stribution dominates retirement\, while the renewable buildout contributes
	 only marginally. The structural estimates and counterfactuals provide a r
	eference point for policymakers and market planners assessing how the disp
	atchable thermal fleet evolves under continued renewable buildout in ERCOT
	. The second chapter extends the analytical framework of Green and Leautie
	r (2015) in two directions. First\, we derive a complete set of marginal i
	mpact results under a Feed-in Premium (FiP)\, an extension they identified
	 as an open problem. Under FiP\, renewable producers receive the wholesale
	 price plus a fixed premium per MWh and curtail output when the price fall
	s below the negative of the premium. The results cover the long-run genera
	tion mix\, the per-MW market value of renewables\, the equilibrium tax\, a
	nd the required marginal subsidy. Because renewables avoid producing in th
	e worst-priced states\, the per-MW market value of renewable capacity fall
	s more slowly as renewable penetration grows under FiP than under a fixed-
	price Feed-in Tariff. Second\, we adapt the framework to the ERCOT market\
	, which uses an Operating Reserve Demand Curve (ORDC) to set scarcity pric
	es in lieu of a forward capacity market. ERCOT's ORDC applies a scarcity a
	dder to the real-time energy price when operating reserves are low\, calib
	rated to the expected cost of the curtailment those reserves would prevent
	. Throughout the chapter's sample period (2019-2023)\, the ORDC operated a
	s an ex post adder to the energy clearing price under sequential energy an
	d ancillary service auctions\, rather than as a co-optimized shadow value 
	within a single security-constrained dispatch. We adopt the economic logic
	 of Hogan (2013)\, who derived the ORDC for a co-optimized setting\, and a
	dapt it to this pre-co-optimization institutional environment. The princip
	al theoretical result is that the ORDC does not directly affect the rate a
	t which renewable per-MW values fall with renewable penetration. Where inf
	ra-marginal conventional generators are at capacity\, marginal changes in 
	reserves shift rents to those generators without altering the rate at whic
	h renewable value declines. The ORDC's effect on renewable values therefor
	e operates indirectly\, through the equilibrium generation mix. A calibrat
	ion exercise using ERCOT data confirms three qualitative findings. The FiP
	 reduces the per-MW subsidy needed to sustain renewable entry and slows th
	e rate at which the per-MW value of renewables falls. Nuclear capacity exi
	ts the equilibrium at approximately 80 GW of wind under FiT and at approxi
	mately 77 GW under ERCOT's ORDC-based market structure\, while the FiP ret
	ains nuclear across the full simulated range. The earlier exit reflects a 
	demand-channel asymmetry that the chapter establishes analytically and the
	 calibration confirms. The combined FiP-ERCOT case is the policy-relevant 
	one for ERCOT\, since the federal Production Tax Credit shares the FiP's p
	remium-on-top-of-price structure. Under this regime\, the calibration show
	s substantially slower per-MW value decline than without the FiP\, with nu
	clear retention preventing the abrupt acceleration that follows nuclear ex
	it. This analysis bridges the analytical framework of Green and Leautier (
	2015) with the scarcity-pricing logic of Hogan (2013)\, and the ERCOT cali
	bration provides its empirical interpretation. The third chapter estimates
	 a discrete choice model of generator investment across ERCOT's four load 
	zones over 2011-2024. Developers choose among five technologies (wind\, so
	lar\, combined-cycle gas\, open-cycle gas\, and battery storage)\, grouped
	 into dispatchable and non-dispatchable segments. Entry composition is est
	imated with a multinomial logit in the Berry (1994) log-odds inversion\, w
	ith operating profit instrumented by national fuel-cost shifters and rival
	 characteristics. Fixed cost deters entry (-3.63\, p &lt\; 0.001) and fede
	ral subsidies shift composition toward the subsidized technologies (+2.29\
	, p &lt\; 0.001). Both results are stable when year and zone fixed effects
	 are added and when the model is re-estimated as a Poisson count model\, w
	hich retains the zone-years with no entry that the log-odds inversion must
	 drop. Entry shows no detectable response to annual energy margins. Gas-fi
	red entry falls well below what observed payoffs alone would predict. The 
	results quantify the mapping from ERCOT's price signals to the technology 
	composition of investment and inform the resource adequacy debate.\n\nhttp
	s://events.rice.edu/live/events/435972-essays-on-the-energy-transition-in-
	electricity\n\nFrom Rice University - Open to the Public
URL:https://events.rice.edu/live/events/435972-essays-on-the-energy-transit
	ion-in-electricity
